194:
No former public servant:
(a) During the twelve (12) months following the date on which he or she ceases to be a public servant, may, for compensation, on behalf of any person other than a governmental entity, make any formal or informal appearance before, or negotiate with, any decision maker in connection with any matter which might give rise to a judicial or quasi-judicial proceeding and which was under the public servant's responsibility as a public servant within twelve (12) months prior to the date on which he or she ceased to be a public servant;
(b) May, for compensation, act on behalf of any party other than the city in connection with any matter in which the former public servant participated personally and substantially as a public servant and which might give rise to a judicial or quasi-judicial proceeding involving the city;
(c) Shall use or continue to use any former official city title, including use on business cards or stationary, following termination of employment with the city.
(Ord. No. 016-13, 7-1-13)

260:
The purpose of this article is to establish a system of personnel administration for the city. The system shall consist of the following merit principles:
(1) Recruiting, selecting and promoting or advancing employees on the basis of their ability, knowledge and skills;
(2) Establishing pay rates or schedules consistent with the principles of providing comparable pay for comparable work;
(3) Retaining employees on the basis of the competency of their job performance, correcting inadequate or incompetent job performance and separating or where possible, reassigning employees whose inadequate or incompetent job performance cannot be corrected; and
(4) Assuring equal opportunity to applicants and employees of the city in all respects without regard to political affiliation, race, creed, color, national origin or ancestry, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, veteran status, religious affiliation or disability.
(Ord. No. 672-77, § 1, 11-21-77; Ord. No. 023-09, § 1, 12-21-09)

262:
The city manager shall be responsible for a personnel system conforming with the policies and principles established by this article. The city manager shall specifically be responsible for:
(1) Administration of the personnel system;
(2) Selection of a personnel director responsible for the technical direction and operation of the personnel system;
(3) Appointment, removal, suspension and discipline of all employees of the City of East Point subject to the policies set forth in this article, the city charter and state and federal law. This authority, at the discretion of the city manager, may be delegated to the head of departments and to supervisory employees for the purpose of effectively recommending personnel action covered by this authority;
(4) The establishment of the number of city employees in the various city departments and offices and determination of duties and compensation of employees in accordance with the policies set forth in this article and subject to budget limitations and approval of the mayor and council; and
(5) Performance of other duties in personnel administration as may be prescribed by law and this article.
(Ord. No. 672-77, § 2, 11-21-77)

263:
The city manager shall appoint or cause to be appointed a personnel director who shall be responsible for the technical direction and operation of the personnel program. The personnel director shall:
(1) Administer, under the direction of the city manager, the personnel system as set forth in this article and the personnel rules and regulations;
(2) Recommend to the city manager personnel rules and regulations;
(3) Maintain the administration of the classification and pay schedule plan;
(4) Develop, coordinate and administer recruiting and training programs for the city and its employees seeking to have the best suited employee for a particular job;
(5) Oversee and administer the maintenance of comprehensive personnel records of all employees of the City of East Point.
(6) Direct and administer the affirmative action program of the City of East Point and furthermore seeking to develop programs creating better employee involvement through awards and incentive programs; and
(7) Recommend changes to the personnel rules and regulations and to this article as necessary from time to time for the effective administration of the personnel system.
(Ord. No. 672-77, § 2, 11-21-77)

264:
The city manager shall present to the city council personnel rules and regulations to be acted upon and approved by the council. The rules and regulations shall cover specific procedures and policies governing the following phases of the personnel system:
(1) Administration of a position classification plan;
(2) Administration of a pay plan;
(3) Announcement of employee vacancies, acceptance of applications and selection methods;
(4) Establishment of promotional policies;
(5) Evaluation of the work of employees, including those serving probationary periods;
(6) Separation from service of employees by resignation, lay-off, suspension or dismissal;
(7) Establishment of work schedules, attendance and leave regulations;
(8) Development of employee training and safety programs;
(9) Rules governing additional or outside employment of city employees;
(10) Uniform system of handling all grievances;
(11) Maintenance and use of necessary records and forms; and
(12) Such other matters as may be necessary to carry out the intent and purpose of this article.
(Ord. No. 672-77, § 2, 11-21-77)

278:
Upon the motion of either party or upon its own motion, the hearing officer or panel may dismiss any appeal under this division prior to holding a full hearing of the appeal upon the following conditions:
(a) The employee failed to exhaust administrative remedies by first appealing to the city manager as provided by section 4-1012(c);
(b) The appeal is clearly moot;
(c) The appeal is without merit;
(d) The appeal is untimely;
(e) The appeal was not properly filed with the city manager; or
(f) The appeal is not within the scope of the hearing officer's/panel's authority.
(Ord. No. 010-08, § 3, 3-17-08)

304:
The personnel director may remove from further consideration the application of an applicant who:
(1) Does not meet the minimum qualifications established for the position;
(2) Has a health handicap that would prevent satisfactory performance of the duties of the position;
(3) Has made false statements of material fact or practiced deception in this application;
(4) Is addicted to the habitual use of drugs or intoxicating liquors;
(5) Has an unsatisfactory employment record of such a nature as to demonstrate unsuitability for employment;
(6) Has been convicted of a crime involving moral turpitude; or
(7) Has failed to submit his application within the prescribed time limit.

326:
Listed below are some reasons which might be cause for disciplinary action referred to in section 4-1065 of this chapter, but disciplinary action is not limited to the offenses listed:
(1) Being convicted of a felony or of a misdemeanor involving moral turpitude;
(2) Being absent without an excused or excusable leave;
(3) Excessive tardiness which is defined as that absence from work which interferes with the efficient performance of job responsibilities and work production;
(4) Abuse of sick leave which is defined as that absence from work which interferes with the efficient performance of job responsibilities and work production;
(5) Inefficient, negligent, incompetent, or total failure in performance of one's duties;
(6) Careless, negligent or improper use of city property or equipment;
(7) Willfully giving false statements to supervisors;
(8) Violation of city ordinances, administrative regulations or departmental rules;
(9) Being under the influence of alcohol or illegal drugs when reporting for work or partaking of such things while on duty, except that prescribed medication may be taken within the limits set by a physician as long as it is medically necessary; or
(10) Instigation of, participation in, or leadership of a strike, work stoppage, slowdown or artificial restriction of productive work.
